{"items":[{"index":47,"prompt":"I’m commissioning a map-style illustration for a small local-history exhibit, so visitors can spot the old Riverside Reading Room from the footpath. Please draw it on a 1920×1200 canvas. I’d like the building to be the clear landmark, with just enough surrounding context—a curving approach path and a simple indication of the riverbank—to make its location understandable. You can decide how the map is framed and arranged; it should feel like a welcoming guide, not a construction plan.

The reading room’s entrance is what I most want people to notice: give its arched doorway, shallow stone steps, lintel, and paired narrow windows distinct, recognizable shapes. Include the roofline and a modest hint of masonry so the facade feels like a real piece of architecture, but don’t let tiny ornament overwhelm its overall form. A small directional mark or brief place label could help if it fits naturally. Keep the setting quiet, without extra buildings or crowds competing with the landmark.

I’ll use this in a printed exhibit handout, so the route and entrance should remain easy to read at a glance. I care about graceful linework, considered contrast, and a few well-placed visual accents; I’m happy to leave the palette and drawing style to your judgment.","skills":["artistic_quality"]}]}
